target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestConvergenceRules'
expect: a_negative_cap_is_still_a_cap
why:    the Python cap guard is a bare `if cap`, which is plain truthiness, so a negative cap is a cap there and any spend clears it. `cap > 0` excludes negative caps and derives halted instead. cmd_plan refuses a non-positive --agent-cap so no new row can carry one, but the store is append-only and never rewritten, so a legacy or hand-written row reads differently in the two implementations.
--- old
	if cap != 0 && spent >= cap {
--- new
	if cap > 0 && spent >= cap {
